<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louisiana Purchase</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1803">1803</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> Purchase was the acquisition by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s territory of Louisiana in 1803 for about 15 million dollars. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> had returned the colony to France shortly before, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> sold it after failing to hold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>. The purchase roughly doubled the size of the United States, though its vague boundaries left disputes such as that over West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louisiana-purchase.jpg" alt="Louisiana Purchase" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Morris · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louisiana_Purchase.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
